        <h2>Mental health in Canada is <em style="color:var(--tw-gold);">at a crossroads</em></h2>
        <hr class="tw-divider tw-divider-left" style="margin-bottom:1.75rem;">
        <p>According to the <a href="https://cmha.ca/find-info/mental-health/general-info/fast-facts/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--tw-gold);">Canadian Mental Health Association</a>, 1 in 5 Canadians personally experience a mental health problem or illness in any given year. Over a lifetime, about 1 in 2 Canadians will have experienced a mental illness. Yet despite how common these experiences are, the stigma around seeking help remains powerful, particularly in communities where silence has been mistaken for strength for generations.</p>
        <p>In Ontario, the average wait time for counselling services reaches 67 days, according to the <a href="https://cmhapeeldufferin.ca/state-of-mental-health-in-canada-report-2024/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--tw-gold);">2024 State of Mental Health in Canada Report</a>. That is 67 days of carrying something alone that you do not have to carry alone. Rates of generalized anxiety disorder have doubled between 2012 and 2022. Major depressive episodes have climbed from 4.7 percent to 7.6 percent of the population.</p>
        <p>These are not just statistics. They are your colleagues, your family members, and perhaps you. The gap between the number of people who need support and the number who receive it is one of the most urgent issues in Canadian healthcare today. Twish exists to help close that gap, one person at a time.</p>
